# FS-UAE Launcher

This is the README file for FS-UAE Launcher.

## Installing FS-UAE Launcher

If you are using the source distribution, you should be able to install
FS-UAE Launcher by running "make install". The setup procedure
requires Python 3 and setuptools to be installed.

## Requirements

FS-UAE Launcher uses Qt 6 through `PySide6`, the `QtPy` compatibility
layer, and `QtAwesome` for scalable dashboard icons. Install the latest
releases supported by your Python version:

  python -m pip install --upgrade PySide6 QtPy QtAwesome requests

The Pipfile also declares these UI dependencies for Pipenv installations.
An existing virtual environment can be updated in place; recreating it is
not necessary:

  venv/bin/python -m pip install --upgrade PySide6 QtPy QtAwesome requests

The UI selects PySide6 by default. Use a clean process without another Qt
binding already imported; PyQt5 and PySide6 must not be mixed. Qt 6 handles
high-DPI scaling automatically. Supported saved themes are preserved; retired
theme names fall back to plain Fusion. Emulator, database, and networking
behavior is unchanged.

It can optionally use the `lhafile` python package to support .lha archives.
The older packaging and INSTALL instructions still target Qt 5 and have not
been migrated as part of this UI-only change. Run from source with the
Python environment containing the Qt 6 dependencies.

## Modern Dark Workspace

Modern Dark is the default when no launcher theme has been selected. The
launcher opens maximized on its current display, with a 1080p-oriented
workspace, a labeled navigation rail, a searchable configuration library,
a resizable configuration editor, and persistent launch controls. All eight
configuration pages remain available. Settings, net play, tools, account
actions, and configuration warnings retain their existing behavior.

The library can be hidden with its toolbar toggle. Quick settings open in a
separate resizable pane, temporarily replacing the library below 1400 pixels
of window width. Screenshots and cover information use the selected
game's existing artwork; the media strip hides below 820 pixels of window
height so editing remains usable. The supported minimum window size is
1000 x 720. Long editor pages scroll rather than forcing a larger window.

Select Modern Dark in the Launcher settings and restart to replace a
previously saved theme. It can also be requested explicitly at startup:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-modern

The four available themes are Modern Dark, Fusion, Windows 3.11, and Amiga
Workbench 1.3. All share this single-window workspace and embedded netplay
server controls. Fusion retains Qt's original light palette and native-style
controls. Removed or unknown saved themes fall back to Fusion; an empty theme
setting still defaults to Modern Dark. Retired saved theme names are normalized
to Fusion so the Settings picker agrees with the running theme. QtAwesome is
installed in the project venv; other source installations must install it as above.

Plain Fusion can also be selected for a single launch:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-plain

Settings uses the same spacious navigation rows, scalable icons, and cyan
selection accent as the main workspace. Its navigation scrolls when needed.
Modern Dark also applies flat, rounded buttons, dropdowns with custom chevrons,
clear keyboard-focus outlines, and larger checkbox/radio indicators throughout
the launcher. These are native Qt controls; no browser or React runtime is
required. The other three themes use their own palette and controls.
Help and netplay information buttons and status-bar notices use the same cyan
icon family as the navigation rail in Modern Dark.

### Quick Theme Switching
Choose a theme in Settings > FS-UAE Launcher, then click Restart Launcher
beside the theme selector. After confirmation, preferences are saved and the
launcher reopens with the selected theme and font. The current base directory
is preserved; old theme overrides and game/disk command-line arguments are
not repeated. Active netplay sessions and hosted servers are stopped. Save
configuration edits before restarting. Cancelling leaves the launcher open.

This is a launcher restart, not a live theme preview: palettes, fonts, and
icon sets are initialized at startup.

### GUI Cleanup

The obsolete standalone netplay/server GUI wrappers and the launcher's
old theme-specific startup, drawing, and resize fallbacks have been removed.
Theme choices now come from one option definition. The tab/book widgets remain
in use by configuration-page construction; shared toolkit window classes and
independently accessible tools are not treated as dead code. Emulator, server
process, database, and network protocol logic is outside this GUI cleanup.

### Retro Themes

The Launcher theme setting also offers Windows 3.11 and Amiga Workbench 1.3.
Select a theme and restart the launcher. Both use the single-window workspace:
Windows 3.11 has gray beveled controls, white fields, and navy selections;
Workbench has a blue background, white square controls, orange selections,
and a monospace font. Fonts fall back to installed alternatives. These are
inspired styles, not emulations; outer window decorations remain controlled
by the operating system. They do not replace any emulator or netplay behavior.
Retro Settings opens at 1100 x 720 to accommodate its wider typography.
Retro navigation uses original, scalable tile treatments around familiar
QtAwesome symbols: teal-topped beveled windows for Windows 3.11 and folded
white tiles with blue/orange details for Workbench. They are modern
reinterpretations, not copies of the original operating systems' artwork.
Settings shares the same icon family, roomy navigation rows, square button
edges, and navy/orange selection colours as its theme's main sidebar. Page
headings use the same typography as the workspace, without large bitmap art.

They can also be selected for a single launch:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-windows311
  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-workbench

### Integrated Netplay

In all four themes, Net play is a page inside the main window rather than a
separate window. The navigation button and Tools
menu open the same page. The retro themes retain their own colours and controls.
Chat, channel selection, and the connection persist when switching back to
configuration pages. Library and quick-settings tools are disabled on the
netplay page, which uses its own host/start controls.

Hosting displays the game ID, channel, port, player count, and server status
alongside the chat. The Stop server button stops hosting without closing
the page. Reset and closing the main window also stop the owned server.
The details update if the server process exits. The panes are resizable;
the chat controls scroll when the window is too narrow to fit them.

## UI Regression Tests

Run the widget, dialog, menu, painting, and screen tests without a display:

    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen python -m unittest discover -s fsui/test -t .

Native file dialogs, multi-monitor placement, and platform-specific appearance
also need interactive testing on Linux, Windows, and macOS.
Image-button regression tests cover icon loading and activation, including
the launcher's New button, under PySide6.
Dashboard tests cover page routing, library/quick-settings toggles, the dark
palette, the theme picker, and 1920x1080, 1280x800, and 1000x720 layouts.
The navigation and embedded-netplay checks run in all four workspace themes,
including chat persistence, inline server ownership, and shutdown on main close.
Settings tests cover navigation icons, page switching, and headings in all
four workspace themes, including the retro themes' larger icons,
palette-based sidebar styling, and matching workspace typography.
Icon-rendering tests cover theme differences and nonblank output at 20, 24,
and 48 pixels in normal, selected, and disabled states.
Control tests verify SVG assets, dropdown selection, checkbox activation,
and numeric stepping.
Theme checks cover saved and command-line retro selection, their palettes,
picker entries, and modern info-icon styling and help-link activation.
Fusion checks cover its light palette, exact four-theme picker, retired-theme
migration, and shared Settings/workspace/server routing.
Restart checks cover confirmation/cancellation, save-before-close ordering,
source/frozen commands, clean launch arguments, shutdown notifications,
and save/relaunch failure handling.
List-selection tests also verify that clearing a search selection emits None
rather than converting it to an incorrect row index under PySide6.
Launch regression tests exercise Start-button dispatch and construction of
the launch task dialog with and without a netplay session. Netplay tests
cover lobby/game-channel and operator/player transitions, button geometry,
and host/send-config/reset/ready/start dispatch without opening a network
connection or launching an emulator. Visibility changes immediately relayout
the netplay controls; no manual resize is required to reveal host buttons.
Real IRC JOIN/NAMES/MODE replay tests cover operator recognition, nick-list
updates, and host controls without mocking operator status. Ordinary IRC
messages use the text widget's current palette color so rendering errors or
an import-time color cannot interrupt IRC events or hide text in dark mode.

## GNOME Startup Compatibility

Some Qt/GNOME combinations abort during startup with a GLib error about the
missing `org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.xsettings` `antialiasing` key.
This is a desktop theme integration failure, not a missing Python package.
On Linux GNOME desktops the UI defaults to Qt's generic platform theme to
avoid that integration. Explicit `QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME` settings are respected;
other desktops and operating systems are unchanged. GNOME-specific theme and
file-dialog integration may differ with the generic platform theme.

To explicitly select the workaround when launching from a terminal:

  QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=generic venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher

## Running FS-UAE Launcher

You start FS-UAE Launcher by running the following command:

    $ fs-uae-launcher

You can also run FS-UAE Launcher without installation by running the
following command in the source root directory:

    $ ./fs-uae-launcher

For an existing virtual environment, explicitly use its Python interpreter:

    $ venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher

